    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In adult life, stem cells are crucial for normal tissue maintenance and repair of damaged tissues. Cell signaling pathways control stem cell activity by regulating self-renewal and proliferation of progenitors that give rise to all cell-types. One such pathway, Hedgehog signaling (Hh), is highly active during embryonic development and important in injury-induced regenerative responses in adult. Hh-hyperactivation has also been implicated in basal-cell carcinoma leading to the use of Hh-antagonist drug, vismodegib, as a cancer therapy. A surprising side-effect of vismodegib is dysguesia, or taste loss, in approximately half of treated patients. This finding, combined with previous results showing ablation of taste receptor cells (TRCs) upon Hh-inhibition, led me to hypothesize that Hh activation in basal lingual epithelial cells is required for maintenance of taste sensation in adult. To test this hypothesis, I optimized a previously described lingual epithelial cell organoid culture system to model proliferation and differentiation of lingual epithelial cells under various culture conditions. I found that while Hh-activation was sufficient to induce organoid population growth, activation of another developmental pathway, BMPR2, severely stunted organoid proliferation. Interestingly, cotreatment with agonists of both Hh and BMPR2 pathways actually enhanced expression of the Hh ligand, sonic hedgehog (Shh). These results imply a complex interaction between developmental pathways in lingual epithelial cells in which Hh-induced cell proliferation may be regulated by other pathways including BMPR2.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
